  <subsection title="Identify Polynomials, Monomials, Binomials and Trinomials">
    <p>You have learned that a term is a constant or the product of a constant and one or more variables. The constant is called a coefficient. When it is of the form $$ax^{m}$$, where $$a$$ is a constant and $$m$$ is a whole number, it is called a monomial. Some examples of monomial are $$8,-2x^{2},4y^{3},\ \text{and}\ 11z^{7}$$.</p>
    <note type="Monomials">
      <p>A monomial is a term of the form $$ax^{m}$$, where $$a$$ is a constant and $$m$$ is a positive whole number.</p>
    </note>
    <p>A monomial, or two or more monomials combined by addition or subtraction, is a polynomial. Some polynomials have special names, based on the number of terms. A monomial is a polynomial with exactly one term. A binomial has exactly two terms, and a trinomial has exactly three terms. There are no special names for polynomials with more than three terms.</p>
    <note type="Polynomials">
      <p>polynomial—A monomial, or two or more monomials combined by addition or subtraction, is a polynomial.</p>
      <list type="ul">
        <item>monomial—A polynomial with exactly one term is called a monomial.</item>
        <item>binomial—A polynomial with exactly two terms is called a binomial.</item>
        <item>trinomial—A polynomial with exactly three terms is called a trinomial.</item>
      </list>
    </note>
    <p>Here are some examples of polynomials.</p>
    <p>$$\begin{array}{lcclcclcclccc} \text{Polynomial} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad b + 1} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 4y^{2} - 7y + 2} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 4x^{4} + x^{3} + 8x^{2} - 9x + 1} &amp; &amp; &amp; \\ \text{Monomial} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 14} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 8y^{2}} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad-9x^{3}y^{5}} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad-13} \\ \text{Binomial} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad a + 7} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 4b - 5} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad y^{2} - 16} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 3x^{3} - 9x^{2}} \\ \text{Trinomial} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad x^{2} - 7x + 12} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 9y^{2} + 2y - 8} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad 6m^{4} - m^{3} + 8m} &amp; &amp; &amp; {\qquad z^{4} + 3z^{2} - 1} \end{array}$$</p>
    <p>Notice that every monomial, binomial, and trinomial is also a polynomial. They are just special members of the “family” of polynomials and so they have special names. We use the words monomial, binomial, and trinomial when referring to these special polynomials and just call all the rest polynomials.</p>
    <example number="6.1">
      <problem>
        <p>Determine whether each polynomial is a monomial, binomial, trinomial, or other polynomial.</p>
        <list type="ol" style="1">
          <item>ⓐ $$4y^{2} - 8y - 6$$</item>
          <item>ⓑ $$-5a^{4}b^{2}$$</item>
          <item>ⓒ $$2x^{5} - 5x^{3} - 9x^{2} + 3x + 4$$</item>
          <item>ⓓ $$13 - 5m^{3}$$</item>
          <item>ⓔ $$q$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Examples of polynomials, showing their number of terms and classification type.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>Polynomial</cell>
              <cell>Number of terms</cell>
              <cell>Type</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ</cell>
              <cell>$$4y^{2} - 8y - 6$$</cell>
              <cell>$$3$$</cell>
              <cell>Trinomial</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ</cell>
              <cell>$$-5a^{4}b^{2}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$1$$</cell>
              <cell>Monomial</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓒ</cell>
              <cell>$$2x^{5} - 5x^{3} - 9x^{2} + 3x + 4$$</cell>
              <cell>$$5$$</cell>
              <cell>Polynomial</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓓ</cell>
              <cell>$$13 - 5m^{3}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$2$$</cell>
              <cell>Binomial</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓔ</cell>
              <cell>$$q$$</cell>
              <cell>$$1$$</cell>
              <cell>Monomial</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.1">
      <p>Determine whether each polynomial is a monomial, binomial, trinomial, or other polynomial:</p>
      <p>ⓐ $$5b$$ ⓑ $$8y^{3} - 7y^{2} - y - 3$$ ⓒ $$-3x^{2} - 5x + 9$$ ⓓ $$81 - 4a^{2}$$ ⓔ $$-5x^{6}$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.2">
      <p>Determine whether each polynomial is a monomial, binomial, trinomial, or other polynomial:</p>
      <p>ⓐ $$27z^{3} - 8$$ ⓑ $$12m^{3} - 5m^{2} - 2m$$ ⓒ $$\frac{5}{6}$$ ⓓ $$8x^{4} - 7x^{2} - 6x - 5$$ ⓔ $$\text{−}n^{4}$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Determine the Degree of Polynomials">
    <p>The degree of a polynomial and the degree of its terms are determined by the exponents of the variable.</p>
    <p>A monomial that has no variable, just a constant, is a special case. The degree of a constant is 0—it has no variable.</p>
    <note type="Degree of a Polynomial">
      <p>The degree of a term is the sum of the exponents of its variables.</p>
      <p>The degree of a constant is 0.</p>
      <p>The degree of a polynomial is the highest degree of all its terms.</p>
    </note>
    <p>Let’s see how this works by looking at several polynomials. We’ll take it step by step, starting with monomials, and then progressing to polynomials with more terms.</p>
    <p>A polynomial is in standard form when the terms of a polynomial are written in descending order of degrees. Get in the habit of writing the term with the highest degree first.</p>
    <example number="6.2">
      <problem>
        <p>Find the degree of the following polynomials.</p>
        <list type="ol" style="1">
          <item>ⓐ $$10y$$</item>
          <item>ⓑ $$4x^{3} - 7x + 5$$</item>
          <item>ⓒ $$-15$$</item>
          <item>ⓓ $$-8b^{2} + 9b - 2$$</item>
          <item>ⓔ $$8xy^{2} + 2y$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates how to determine the degree of various mathematical expressions, including monomials, polynomials, and constants.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓐ
The exponent of $$y$$ is one. $$y = y^{1}$$</cell>
              <cell>$$10y$$
The degree is 1.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓑ
The highest degree of all the terms is 3.</cell>
              <cell>$$4x^{3} - 7x + 5$$
The degree is 3.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓒ
The degree of a constant is 0.</cell>
              <cell>$$-15$$
The degree is 0.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓓ
The highest degree of all the terms is 2.</cell>
              <cell>$$-8b^{2} + 9b - 2$$
The degree is 2.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>ⓔ
The highest degree of all the terms is 3.</cell>
              <cell>$$8xy^{2} + 2y$$
The degree is 3.</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.3">
      <p>Find the degree of the following polynomials:</p>
      <p>ⓐ $$-15b$$ ⓑ $$10z^{4} + 4z^{2} - 5$$ ⓒ $$12c^{5}d^{4} + 9c^{3}d^{9} - 7$$ ⓓ $$3x^{2}y - 4x$$ ⓔ $$-9$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.4">
      <p>Find the degree of the following polynomials:</p>
      <p>ⓐ $$52$$ ⓑ $$a^{4}b - 17a^{4}$$ ⓒ $$5x + 6y + 2z$$ ⓓ $$3x^{2} - 5x + 7$$ ⓔ $$\text{−}a^{3}$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Add and Subtract Monomials">
    <p>You have learned how to simplify expressions by combining like terms. Remember, like terms must have the same variables with the same exponent. Since monomials are terms, adding and subtracting monomials is the same as combining like terms. If the monomials are like terms, we just combine them by adding or subtracting the coefficient.</p>
    <example number="6.3">
      <problem>
        <p>Add: $$25y^{2} + 15y^{2}$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the process of combining like terms in an algebraic expression.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$25y^{2} + 15y^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$40y^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.5">
      <p>Add: $$12q^{2} + 9q^{2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.6">
      <p>Add: $$-15c^{2} + 8c^{2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.4">
      <problem>
        <p>Subtract: $$16p - \left( {-7p} \right)$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="This table demonstrates the step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression by combining like terms.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$16p - \left( {-7p} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$23p$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.7">
      <p>Subtract: $$8m - \left( {-5m} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.8">
      <p>Subtract: $$-15z^{3} - \left( {-5z^{3}} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Remember that like terms must have the same variables with the same exponents.</p>
    <example number="6.5">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$c^{2} + 7d^{2} - 6c^{2}$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Demonstrates simplifying an algebraic expression by combining like terms.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$c^{2} + 7d^{2} - 6c^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$-5c^{2} + 7d^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.9">
      <p>Add: $$8y^{2} + 3z^{2} - 3y^{2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.10">
      <p>Add: $$3m^{2} + n^{2} - 7m^{2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.6">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$u^{2}v + 5u^{2} - 3v^{2}$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="An algebraic expression and a note on its like terms.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$u^{2}v + 5u^{2} - 3v^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>There are no like terms to combine.</cell>
              <cell>$$u^{2}v + 5u^{2} - 3v^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.11">
      <p>Simplify: $$m^{2}n^{2} - 8m^{2} + 4n^{2}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.12">
      <p>Simplify: $$pq^{2} - 6p - 5q^{2}.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Add and Subtract Polynomials">
    <p>We can think of adding and subtracting polynomials as just adding and subtracting a series of monomials. Look for the like terms—those with the same variables and the same exponent. The Commutative Property allows us to rearrange the terms to put like terms together.</p>
    <example number="6.7">
      <problem>
        <p>Find the sum: $$\left( {5y^{2} - 3y + 15} \right) + \left( {3y^{2} - 4y - 11} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell>Identify like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rearrange to get the like terms together.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.13">
      <p>Find the sum: $$\left( {7x^{2} - 4x + 5} \right) + \left( {x^{2} - 7x + 3} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.14">
      <p>Find the sum: $$\left( {14y^{2} + 6y - 4} \right) + \left( {3y^{2} + 8y + 5} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.8">
      <problem>
        <p>Find the difference: $$\left( {9w^{2} - 7w + 5} \right) - \left( {2w^{2} - 4} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute and identify like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rearrange the terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.15">
      <p>Find the difference: $$\left( {8x^{2} + 3x - 19} \right) - \left( {7x^{2} - 14} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.16">
      <p>Find the difference: $$\left( {9b^{2} - 5b - 4} \right) - \left( {3b^{2} - 5b - 7} \right).$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.9">
      <problem>
        <p>Subtract: $$\left( {c^{2} - 4c + 7} \right)$$ from $$\left( {7c^{2} - 5c + 3} \right)$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute and identify like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rearrange the terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.17">
      <p>Subtract: $$\left( {5z^{2} - 6z - 2} \right)$$ from $$\left( {7z^{2} + 6z - 4} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.18">
      <p>Subtract: $$\left( {x^{2} - 5x - 8} \right)$$ from $$\left( {6x^{2} + 9x - 1} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.10">
      <problem>
        <p>Find the sum: $$\left( {u^{2} - 6uv + 5v^{2}} \right) + \left( {3u^{2} + 2uv} \right)$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step addition of two polynomial expressions, showing distribution, rearrangement, and combining like terms for simplification.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\left( {u^{2} - 6uv + 5v^{2}} \right) + \left( {3u^{2} + 2uv} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell>$$u^{2} - 6uv + 5v^{2} + 3u^{2} + 2uv$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rearrange the terms, to put like terms together.</cell>
              <cell>$$u^{2} + 3u^{2} - 6uv + 2uv + 5v^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$4u^{2} - 4uv + 5v^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.19">
      <p>Find the sum: $$\left( {3x^{2} - 4xy + 5y^{2}} \right) + \left( {2x^{2} - xy} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.20">
      <p>Find the sum: $$\left( {2x^{2} - 3xy - 2y^{2}} \right) + \left( {5x^{2} - 3xy} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.11">
      <problem>
        <p>Find the difference: $$\left( {p^{2} + q^{2}} \right) - \left( {p^{2} + 10pq - 2q^{2}} \right)$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step algebraic simplification of the expression (p^2 + q^2) - (p^2 + 10pq - 2q^2).">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\left( {p^{2} + q^{2}} \right) - \left( {p^{2} + 10pq - 2q^{2}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell>$$p^{2} + q^{2} - p^{2} - 10pq + 2q^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rearrange the terms, to put like terms together.</cell>
              <cell>$$p^{2} - p^{2} - 10pq + q^{2} + 2q^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$-10pq + 3q^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.21">
      <p>Find the difference: $$\left( {a^{2} + b^{2}} \right) - \left( {a^{2} + 5ab - 6b^{2}} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.22">
      <p>Find the difference: $$\left( {m^{2} + n^{2}} \right) - \left( {m^{2} - 7mn - 3n^{2}} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <example number="6.12">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: $$\left( {a^{3} - a^{2}b} \right) - \left( {ab^{2} + b^{3}} \right) + \left( {a^{2}b + ab^{2}} \right)$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table summary="Step-by-step simplification of an algebraic expression through distribution, rearrangement, and combining like terms.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\left( {a^{3} - a^{2}b} \right) - \left( {ab^{2} + b^{3}} \right) + \left( {a^{2}b + ab^{2}} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell>$$a^{3} - a^{2}b - ab^{2} - b^{3} + a^{2}b + ab^{2}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Rearrange the terms, to put like terms together.</cell>
              <cell>$$a^{3} - a^{2}b + a^{2}b - ab^{2} + ab^{2} - b^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
              <cell>$$a^{3} - b^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.23">
      <p>Simplify: $$\left( {x^{3} - x^{2}y} \right) - \left( {xy^{2} + y^{3}} \right) + \left( {x^{2}y + xy^{2}} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="6.24">
      <p>Simplify: $$\left( {p^{3} - p^{2}q} \right) + \left( {pq^{2} + q^{3}} \right) - \left( {p^{2}q + pq^{2}} \right)$$.</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Evaluate a Polynomial for a Given Value">
    <p>We have already learned how to evaluate expressions. Since polynomials are expressions, we’ll follow the same procedures to evaluate a polynomial. We will substitute the given value for the variable and then simplify using the order of operations.</p>
